---
// Methodology (English): the same structure and figures as /methodology.
// The calculations are shared (lib/methodology.ts); wording follows METHODOLOGY_EN.md of the dataset where the content matches.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import wave from '../../data/wave-2026.json';
import verification from '../../data/verification-2026.json';
import { indexApi, type Kind } from '../../lib/indices';
import { DOI_URL, SAMPLE_ARGUMENT_EN } from '../../lib/site';
import { rep, compByCrit, biasCls, W, sums, sumMax, nearSum, assessCls } from '../../lib/methodology';
import { i18n } from '../../i18n';

const { lang, tr, link, num, signed, pval, int, pct } = i18n(Astro);
const { INDICES, KIND } = indexApi(lang);

const w = wave.wave;
const v = verification;

// Sample profile: shares from the dataset METHODOLOGY, section 3 (these characteristics were optional)
const profile = [
  { label: 'Position', detail: 'research-and-teaching staff 47.0%; heads / deputy heads of units 21.5%; research staff (without teaching) 19.0%; PhD students 4.5%' },
  { label: 'Academic degree', detail: 'hold a degree 87.5% (Candidate of Sciences / PhD 63.5%; Doctor of Sciences 24.0%); no degree 12.5%' },
  { label: 'Academic title', detail: 'Associate Professors 37.0%; no academic title 34.0%; Professors 17.5%; Senior Researchers / Senior Research Fellows 10.5%; other statuses 1.0%' },
  { label: 'Years in research', detail: 'mostly over 10 years; the 11–20 and 21–30 year groups together make up 56.5%' },
  { label: 'Field of science', detail: 'the most represented are the social sciences (27.6%) and natural sciences and mathematics (24.1%), self-assessed' },
  { label: 'Type of research', detail: 'applied research 58.9%; basic research 51.3%; scientific and technical development 15.2% (multiple choice)' },
  { label: 'Type of institution', detail: 'HEIs 70.4%; research institutions 28.6%; other 1%' },
  { label: 'Geography', detail: '21 regions of Ukraine; most of all Kyiv region (32.1%) and Odesa region (18.4%)' },
];

const kinds: Kind[] = ['state', 'change', 'expect'];
const ofKind = (k: Kind) => INDICES.filter((i) => i.kind === k);

// the two summary rows reworded for the site, as on the Ukrainian page
const rc = v.reverseCheck;
const SUMMARY_TEXT: Record<string, { dimension: string; result: string }> = {
  'Узгодженість оцінок часу': {
    dimension: 'Consistency of working time structure estimates',
    result: `sum of the three shares: mean ${pct(v.shareSumCheck.mean)}; median ${pct(v.shareSumCheck.median)}`,
  },
  'Якість і узгодженість відповідей': {
    dimension: 'Consistency of answers to related questions',
    result: `among those for whom MA takes up more than ${rc.threshold}% of time (n = ${rc.n}), only ${rc.notImprove} (${pct(rc.pct)}) thinks that removing it would not improve the quality of work`,
  },
};
const summary = v.psychometricSummary.ua.map((r, i) => ({
  ...v.psychometricSummary.en[i],
  cls: assessCls(r.assessment),
  ...SUMMARY_TEXT[r.dimension],
}));
const CAT = { НД: 'Actual scientific (ASA)', КН: 'Useful non-scientific (UNSA)', БД: 'Meaningless (MA)' } as Record<string, string>;
---

<Layout title="Methodology — Scientists Barometer" description="How the indices are calculated, the three types of indicators, uncertainty, the sample, and the reliability and validity of the instrument.">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The full version is in the documentation of the <a href={DOI_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">dataset</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#indeks">How the index is calculated</a></li>
      <li><a href="#typy">Three types of indicators</a></li>
      <li><a href="#vybirka">Sample and uncertainty</a></li>
      <li><a href="#profil">Sample profile</a></li>
      <li><a href="#reprezentatyvnist">Representativeness</a></li>
      <li><a href="#pidhotovka">Data preparation</a></li>
      <li><a href="#nadiinist">Reliability and validity</a></li>
      <li><a href="#etyka">Ethics and data protection</a></li>
      <li><a href="#mezhi">Limits</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3 id="indeks">1. How the index is calculated</h3>
    <p>
      The indices are built from researchers’ own structured assessments and combined into a
      <b>diffusion index</b>, by analogy with the Purchasing Managers’ Index (PMI) and the consumer
      sentiment index.
    </p>
    <div class="formula">
      <code>Index = [P₁·1 + P₂·0.5 + P₃·0 + P₄·(−0.5) + P₅·(−1)] · 100 + 100</code>
      <p class="note" style="margin:0.5rem 0 0">
        P₁…P₅ are the shares of responses from the most positive to the most negative. The range is
        <b>0–200</b>; <b>100 = neutral level</b>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      <b>The 95% confidence interval (CI)</b> shows the precision of an estimate: the range of index values
      compatible with the responses obtained, given that not everyone was surveyed. It is narrower the more
      responses there are (here from 188 to 199 per question) and the smaller their spread.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The composite <b>Research Capacity Index (RCI)</b> is the geometric mean of the three adequacy indices
      (funding, equipment, time). Its CI was computed by bootstrap: 10,000 times, a new sample of the same
      size was drawn at random from the same respondents (one person could enter it several times, another
      not at all), the RCI was recalculated for each, and the CI limits are the values between which 95% of
      the results lie. This takes into account that the three components of the RCI are related to one
      another.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same index value can mean different things: 100 can be “almost everyone chose the neutral
      response” or “opinions were split in half”. That is why the site always shows the
      <b>response distribution</b> next to the index.
    </p>

    <h3 id="typy">2. Three types of indicators</h3>
    <p>
      The 24 indices measure three different things, and the site presents them separately:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th class="num">Indices</th><th>Which</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {kinds.map((k) => (
          <tr>
            <td><b>{KIND[k].label}</b></td>
            <td>{KIND[k].note}</td>
            <td class="num">{ofKind(k).length}</td>
            <td class="note">{ofKind(k).map((i) => i.code).join(', ')}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <b>Assessed change is not measured change.</b> Respondents said how, in their view, the share of time,
      adequacy of resources or quality had changed over one year and five years. This is their assessment
      at the time of the survey. Retrospective indices are not included in the construct validity test
      (section 7).
    </p>

    <h3 id="vybirka">3. Sample and uncertainty</h3>
    <p>
      <b>N = {w.n}</b> (25.02.2026 – 13.04.2026, {w.field.days} days; most responses came in March). The
      population is <b>{int(w.population.size)}</b> research, research-and-teaching and teaching staff of
      higher education institutions (HEIs) and research institutions (State Statistics Service of Ukraine,
      2024); the sample is {pct(w.population.fractionPct, 2)}. The questionnaire was on Google Forms,
      distributed through three channels at once: invitations to addresses from the institutions’ official
      sites, requests to the heads of institutions, and social networks and research communities. Selection
      was <b>non-random</b> (convenience / self-selection); participation was voluntary and anonymous.
    </p>
    <p>
      Uncertainty is shown with <b>95% confidence intervals</b>: they describe the statistical precision of
      the estimates. How far the sample differs from the population was assessed with the χ² test
      (section 5).
    </p>
    <h4>Confidence intervals in demographic breakdowns</h4>
    <p>
      A <b>breakdown</b> divides respondents by one characteristic: field, degree, title, type of
      institution, years in research or region. A <b>group</b> is one category of a breakdown, for example
      the “Social sciences” field or “HEI”.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the heat maps on the block pages, every cell has a CI (the “Show 95% CI” switch):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>shares</b> (% choosing, agreeing, with inadequate provision): the Wilson interval. Unlike the
        usual “share ± 1.96·√(p(1−p)/n)”, it does not go beyond 0–100% and is correct for small n and for
        shares near the edge, which is why it is asymmetric near 0 or 100% (e.g. 93.4% → 89.1–96.1). The CIs
        of the four percentage indicators on the home page are computed the same way;</li>
      <li><b>indices</b>: as for the overall indices, but using only the responses of people in the group:
        the index value in the group ± 1.96 standard errors, which depend on the spread of responses in the
        group and their number. The limits are constrained to the 0–200 scale;</li>
      <li><b>mean shares of time</b>: the group mean ± 1.96 standard errors of the mean (likewise based on
        the spread and number of responses in the group).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <b>The smaller the group, the wider the interval.</b> Each cell is computed only from the responses of
      people in that group to the relevant question, and groups can be very small, from 5 people.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>In groups with fewer than 20 responses, a typical 95% CI for shares is about
        {Math.round(W.small)} points wide (roughly ±{Math.round(W.small / 2)}); in larger groups, about
        {Math.round(W.large)} (±{Math.round(W.large / 2)}). So small differences between groups may be due to
        chance.</li>
      <li>Cells with fewer than 20 responses are hatched in the heat maps.</li>
      <li>For indices in small groups the CI is only approximate.</li>
      <li>If everyone in a group answered identically, the formula gives an interval of zero width. This
        does not mean perfect precision, so such a CI is not shown: the cell displays “—”.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      CIs in breakdowns were computed from the primary data and are not part of version 1.0 of the dataset on
      Zenodo, so they cannot be reproduced from the dataset (the point values can).
    </p>

    <h3 id="profil">4. Sample profile</h3>
    <p>
      Questions about respondents’ characteristics were <b>optional</b> in the questionnaire.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Sample composition</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {profile.map((p) => (
          <tr><td><b>{p.label}</b></td><td>{p.detail}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      Type of institution, degree, title and field are compared with the population in more detail below;
      the shares there are based on those who answered the relevant question, so they may differ slightly.
    </p>

    <h3 id="reprezentatyvnist">5. Representativeness (χ² against the State Statistics Service)</h3>
    <p>
      The sample was compared with the population on four criteria. On all of them the difference is
      statistically significant (p &lt; 0.05). The bias is systematic: the sample is skewed towards more
      qualified respondents and those more engaged in research. Such bias is typical of surveys of this
      kind. {SAMPLE_ARGUMENT_EN} Region is descriptive only: there are too few observations in the regions
      for χ².
    </p>
    {rep.criteria.map((cr) => (
      <section style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        <h2 style="margin-bottom:0.3rem">{tr(cr.criterion)}</h2>
        <p class="note" style="margin:0 0 0.4rem">
          χ² = {num(cr.chi2, 2)}, df = {cr.df}, p = {pval(cr.p)} (N = {cr.n})
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Category</th><th class="num">population %</th><th class="num">sample %</th><th class="num">bias, pp</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {compByCrit(cr.criterion).map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{tr(r.category)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{num(r.popPct)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{num(r.samplePct)}</td>
                  <td class={`num ${biasCls(r.biasPp)}`}>{signed(r.biasPp)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}

    <h3 id="pidhotovka">6. Data preparation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cleaning the sample.</b> The initial database contained 202 questionnaires; two were excluded
        (one from outside the target geography, one from a respondent outside the target group). The final
        sample is 200.</li>
      <li><b>Missing answers. Questions were optional</b>, so each indicator is computed on those who
        answered the relevant question; n is given beside each value.</li>
      <li><b>Time shares</b> were chosen by respondents as intervals (0–10%, 11–20% … 91–100%); the
        calculations use the interval midpoints (5, 15 … 95%).</li>
      <li><b>Categorisation.</b> The <a href={link('/about#kategorii')}>ASA / UNSA / MA</a> shares are
        computed from the sum of these three answers; “hard to say” and “not encountered” are not part of
        the base.</li>
      <li><b>Fields.</b> Non-standard answers (in particular interdisciplinary ones) were merged into
        “Other (interdisciplinary)”.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="nadiinist">7. Reliability and validity</h3>
    <h4>Summary assessment</h4>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Method</th><th>Result</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {summary.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><b>{r.dimension}</b></td>
              <td>{r.method}</td>
              <td>{r.result}</td>
              <td><span class={`assess ${r.cls}`}>{r.assessment}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4>Internal consistency of scales (Cronbach α and MIC)</h4>
    <p>
      Cronbach α shows how consistently the items of a scale measure a common construct. MIC is the mean
      correlation between all pairs of items; unlike α, it does not depend on the number of items, so it is
      the right measure for comparing scales of different lengths.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Scale</th><th class="num">Items</th><th class="num">N</th><th class="num">α</th><th class="num">MIC</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {v.scaleStatistics.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{tr(s.scale)}</td>
              <td class="num">{s.k}</td>
              <td class="num">{s.n}</td>
              <td class="num"><b>{num(s.alpha, 3)}</b></td>
              <td class="num">{num(s.mic, 3)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      N is the number of respondents with complete answers to all items of the scale. By Nunnally’s
      criterion, α ≥ 0.70 is acceptable, ≥ 0.80 good and ≥ 0.90 excellent: all seven scales are acceptable or
      better, four are good; all MIC ≥ 0.50.
    </p>
    <ul class="note">
      <li>The highest α, for the expectations scale, does not mean it is the “most reliable”: α rises
        mechanically with the number of items. By MIC the most closely related items are those on
        expectations (0.838) and on the quality of others’ research (0.712), and the weakest those on adequacy
        of resources (0.510). A very high MIC can also indicate redundancy of individual items.</li>
      <li>The quality scales combine the current level with assessed change over 1 year and 5 years, which
        are conceptually different dimensions. They correlate closely enough to be combined but are not
        strictly unidimensional: α tests consistency, not unidimensionality.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Rater agreement (Fleiss κ)</h4>
    <p>
      How uniformly researchers assign activities to the actual scientific / useful non-scientific /
      meaningless categories: <b>κ = {num(wave.reliability.fleiss.kappa, 3)}</b> (mean agreement
      P̄ = {num(wave.reliability.fleiss.pBar, 3)}), “fair” on the Landis–Koch scale. This level of consensus is
      to be expected: the boundaries between the categories are partly subjective, and the degree of
      disagreement is itself a substantive result. Agreement for each activity (Pᵢ) is on the
      <a href={link('/categorization')}>categorisation</a> page.
    </p>

    <h4>Construct validity (Spearman ρ)</h4>
    <p>
      Whether theoretically related constructs correlate in the expected direction: more actual scientific
      activity and more resources → higher quality and more optimistic expectations; more meaningless
      activity → lower. Only current and prospective measures, taken at the same time, were used.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Construct pair</th><th class="num">N</th><th class="num">ρ</th><th class="num">p</th><th class="num">p (BH)</th><th class="num">p (BY)</th><th>Direction</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {v.constructPairs.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{tr(p.pair)}</td>
              <td class="num">{p.n}</td>
              <td class={`num ${p.rho >= 0 ? 'bias-pos' : 'bias-neg'}`}><b>{signed(p.rho, 3)}</b></td>
              <td class="num">{pval(p.p)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pval(p.pBH)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pval(p.pBY)}</td>
              <td>{tr(p.expected)}, {p.directionMatch === 'так' ? 'as expected' : 'not as expected'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Research capacity (score) is the individual sum of the three adequacy ratings (3–15), not the composite
      index. p (BH) and p (BY) are the Benjamini–Hochberg correction and the stricter Benjamini–Yekutieli
      correction for five comparisons. Four relationships are significant under BH and three under BY. By
      Cohen’s criteria two correlations are moderate and the rest weak; for distinct constructs this is to
      be expected: correlations that were too high would mean the constructs do not differ. The strongest
      relationship is between the share of actual scientific activity and the quality of own research
      (ρ = +0.351).
    </p>

    <h4>Known groups: HEIs and research institutions (Mann–Whitney test)</h4>
    <p>
      The structure of working time should differ between higher education institutions and research
      institutions, where research is the main activity. The test is non-parametric because the responses
      are intervals, the distributions are skewed and the groups are of different sizes.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th class="num">HEI: mean / median (N)</th><th class="num">RI: mean / median (N)</th><th class="num">Difference, pp</th><th class="num">U</th><th class="num">p</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {v.knownGroups.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{CAT[g.category]}</td>
              <td class="num">{num(g.zvo.mean)} / {num(g.zvo.median)} ({g.zvo.n})</td>
              <td class="num">{num(g.nu.mean)} / {num(g.nu.median)} ({g.nu.n})</td>
              <td class="num">{signed(g.delta)}</td>
              <td class="num">{num(g.U, g.U % 1 ? 1 : 0)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pval(g.p)}{g.p >= 0.05 ? ' (n.s.)' : ''}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In research institutions the share of actual scientific activity is substantially higher and the
      share of meaningless activity lower: the instrument distinguishes groups that ought to differ.
      Distributions by type of institution are on the
      <a href={link('/time-structure#zvo-nu')}>working time structure</a> page.
    </p>

    <h4>Consistency of answers</h4>
    <p>
      <b>Sum-of-shares check.</b> Respondents assessed the share of working time on actual scientific, useful
      non-scientific and meaningless activity in three separate questions, each by choosing an interval
      (0–10%, 11–20% … 91–100%). The questionnaire did not require the three answers to add up to 100%, so
      their sum is an independent check of how attentively it was filled in. For each respondent the three
      shares were taken as the midpoints of the chosen intervals (5, 15 … 95%) and added. Each midpoint can
      differ from the true share by up to 5 pp, so the sum of the three can differ by up to 15 pp: with sums
      from 85 to 115% the true sum could have been exactly 100%.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mean sum is {pct(v.shareSumCheck.mean)} and the median {pct(v.shareSumCheck.median)}
      (N = {v.shareSumCheck.n}); {nearSum} respondents ({pct((nearSum / v.shareSumCheck.n) * 100)}) fall in
      the 85–115% range.
    </p>
    <div class="sumhist" role="img" aria-label="Distribution of the sums of the three time shares">
      {sums.map((d) => (
        <div class={`sh-col${d.sum >= 85 && d.sum <= 115 ? ' near' : ''}`} title={`sum ${d.sum}%: ${d.n} respondents`}>
          <span class="sh-bar" style={`height:${(d.n / sumMax) * 100}%`}></span>
          <span class="sh-x">{d.sum}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="note">Horizontal axis: sum of the three shares (interval midpoints, %); vertical axis: number of respondents. 85–115% is highlighted.</p>
    <p>
      <b>Reverse check.</b> Among the {rc.n} respondents for whom meaningless activity takes up more than
      {rc.threshold}% of working time, only {rc.notImprove} ({pct(rc.pct)}) thinks that removing it would not
      improve the quality of their work
      ({rc.answers.filter((a) => a.n > 0).map((a) => `${a.n} said “${tr(a.label).toLowerCase()}”`).join(', ')}).
      The answers are consistent with the study’s central claim, which indicates that the questionnaire was
      filled in thoughtfully.
    </p>

    <h3 id="etyka">8. Ethics and data protection</h3>
    <p>
      Before filling in the questionnaire, every respondent gave mandatory consent to take part in the study
      and to the processing of their data. All other questions were optional, no payment was offered and no
      email addresses were collected. Circulating the questionnaire through the heads of institutions in the
      southern region was supported by a letter from the Southern Scientific Centre of the National Academy
      of Sciences of Ukraine and the Ministry of Education and Science of Ukraine. The open verification
      questions (position and institution) were used only to verify responses and have been deleted.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Aggregates only.</b> Microdata (the responses of individual respondents) are not published.</li>
      <li><b>Suppression of small groups.</b> Groups with n &lt; 5 are not shown in breakdowns (“·”), nor are
        individual cells where fewer than 5 people in a group answered the question.</li>
      <li><b>Breakdowns are one-dimensional:</b> each demographic characteristic is shown separately, with no
        cross-tabulations, so an individual respondent cannot be “reconstructed” by intersecting
        characteristics. Resistance to recovering suppressed groups by subtraction has been checked.</li>
      <li><b>Comments</b> are given verbatim and unedited in the Ukrainian version and in translation here;
        they were checked for direct identifiers (names, contacts, references to a specific person), and
        there are none.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="mezhi">9. Limits</h3>
    <p>
      The sample is skewed towards more qualified and more research-engaged respondents (section 5); whether
      to generalise the results in view of this bias is for the user of the data to decide. The aim of the
      survey is to test whether intersubjective indices for assessing the state of the research system can
      be measured at system level.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Known limitations of the instrument: two respondents said in comments that some answer buttons did not
      work (with no noticeable loss of responses); the questionnaire did not allow several fields to be
      chosen at once; some respondents note that they estimated time shares approximately.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
